WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.120
Some pathogens kill the very cells they infect, and viruses often do.

2
00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.280
That arrow from the first film ran one way, from host to pathogen.

3
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.820
But when a virus kills a cell, where does that cell's matter go?

4
00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.490
Picture a virus bursting a cell in the ocean.

5
00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.950
A moment ago, that cell held nitrogen.

6
00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:27.770
Where is that nitrogen now?

7
00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:31.903
Ocean water holds enormous numbers of viruses.

8
00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:38.083
A virus that infects bacteria is called a bacteriophage, or phage for short.

9
00:00:39.443 --> 00:00:41.463
Other ocean viruses infect algae.

10
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:45.147
Researchers built a lab model ecosystem.

11
00:00:46.607 --> 00:00:50.707
It was a flask of seawater with an ocean alga called Phaeocystis.

12
00:00:51.987 --> 00:00:53.947
Then they added a virus that infects it.

13
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.120
The viruses infected the algae.

14
00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.380
Then the cells burst, spilling much of what was inside them into the water.

15
00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:04.660
The algae were dead.

16
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:07.400
Was their nitrogen gone too?

17
00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:13.170
No. Bacteria swarmed the remains and broke them down.

18
00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:19.190
The water gained nitrogen and phosphorus, in forms other living things could use.

19
00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.510
Algae and bacteria can take them up.

20
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.490
So the nitrogen was not destroyed.

21
00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:31.090
The virus killed the cell, but much of the cell's matter went back into the water.

22
00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:35.010
A killer can also be part of a recycling chain.

23
00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:40.770
Virus, then bacteria, then other living things: each step moves matter on.

24
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.080
Now zoom out to a whole landscape, the Serengeti in East Africa.

25
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:52.580
Rinderpest is a virus disease of cattle and of wild grazing animals, such as

26
00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:53.580
wildebeest.

27
00:01:54.233 --> 00:01:59.353
In the 1960s, vaccinating cattle wiped rinderpest out of the Serengeti.

28
00:02:00.493 --> 00:02:01.633
The virus was gone.

29
00:02:02.993 --> 00:02:04.093
What happened to the land?

30
00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:07.013
Here is a question to try.

31
00:02:08.073 --> 00:02:12.653
With rinderpest gone, will the Serengeti end up with more trees, or with fewer

32
00:02:12.653 --> 00:02:13.653
trees?

33
00:02:19.267 --> 00:02:25.587
More trees. Researchers traced the chain using decades of records and computer

34
00:02:25.587 --> 00:02:29.227
models. First, wildebeest numbers rose.

35
00:02:30.587 --> 00:02:32.187
More wildebeest ate more grass.

36
00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:35.907
Less dry grass meant less fuel for fires.

37
00:02:37.247 --> 00:02:40.027
Fewer fires burned, so more young trees survived.

38
00:02:41.367 --> 00:02:45.847
Their analysis supported this chain, from the loss of one virus to more trees.

39
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.620
Two scales, two lessons.

40
00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:54.420
In the flask, matter from the dead cells went back into the water for other living

41
00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:60.000
things. On the savanna, losing one virus changed which populations grew.

42
00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.040
Viruses that burst cells can recycle matter.

43
00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:06.980
Pathogens can reshape ecosystems.
